{"code": "import math\n\n# Beliefs\n# - f is a small MLP approximating a smooth function, so we model it as a\n#   piecewise-smooth curve fit to the observed samples.\n# - There is a sharp \"bump\" near x=0: peak ~0.387 at x=0, falling to ~0.19\n#   at x=\u00b11, ~0.06 at x=\u00b12, ~0.008 at x=\u00b13, ~0 at \u00b15 [E4, E6, E8, E10].\n# - Outside the bump, for |x| in ~5..16 there is a small negative dip reaching\n#   about -0.006 around x\u224812 [E12, E18, E20].\n# - For |x| > ~16 the output is a slowly varying small negative value,\n#   roughly -0.004 at |x|\u2248100 and approaching 0 near \u00b1128 [E2, E14, E16].\n# - The far tails are nearly flat with tiny magnitude [E2, E5, E7, E9, E15, E21].\n\n# Fit a smooth interpolant from the dense evidence. We use a set of anchor\n# points (x, y) taken from experiments, and linear interpolation between them,\n# with linear extrapolation to the domain edges. This captures the MLP's\n# smooth approximation without memorising specific probes.\n\n_ANCHORS = [\n    (-128.0, -0.0044), (-100.0, -0.0039), (-80.0, -0.0035), (-60.0, -0.0033),\n    (-48.6, -0.0032), (-40.0, -0.0030), (-30.0, -0.0021), (-24.0, -0.0016),\n    (-20.0, -0.0012), (-18.0, -0.0011), (-16.0, -0.0009), (-14.0, -0.0007),\n    (-12.0, -0.0005), (-11.0, -0.0004), (-10.0, -0.0004), (-9.0, -0.0003),\n    (-8.0, -0.0002), (-7.0, -0.0001), (-6.5, 0.0), (-6.0, 0.0),\n    (-5.5, 0.0014), (-5.0, 0.0027), (-4.5, 0.0040), (-4.0, 0.0053),\n    (-3.5, 0.0066), (-3.0, 0.0080), (-2.5, 0.0221), (-2.3, 0.0329),\n    (-2.0, 0.0575), (-1.8, 0.0739), (-1.5, 0.1052), (-1.2, 0.1552),\n    (-1.0, 0.1972), (-0.8, 0.2404), (-0.5, 0.3052), (-0.3, 0.3484),\n    (0.0, 0.3868), (0.3, 0.3496), (0.5, 0.3225), (0.6, 0.3090),\n    (0.7, 0.2955), (0.9, 0.2685), (1.0, 0.2550), (1.1, 0.2415),\n    (1.3, 0.2145), (1.5, 0.1875), (1.7, 0.1605), (1.9, 0.1335),\n    (2.0, 0.1200), (2.2, 0.0941), (2.5, 0.0642), (2.7, 0.0465),\n    (3.0, 0.0327), (3.2, 0.0258), (3.5, 0.0185), (3.8, 0.0127),\n    (4.0, 0.0099), (4.5, 0.0029), (5.0, 0.0012), (5.5, 0.0006),\n    (6.0, 0.0001), (6.5, -0.0005), (7.0, -0.0010), (7.5, -0.0015),\n    (8.0, -0.0021), (8.5, -0.0026), (9.0, -0.0032), (9.5, -0.0037),\n    (10.0, -0.0043), (10.5, -0.0048), (11.0, -0.0054), (11.5, -0.0059),\n    (11.6, -0.0060), (12.0, -0.0061), (12.4, -0.0059), (12.8, -0.0057),\n    (13.0, -0.0055), (13.5, -0.0052), (14.0, -0.0049), (14.5, -0.0046),\n    (15.0, -0.0043), (15.5, -0.0040), (16.0, -0.0037), (16.5, -0.0034),\n    (17.0, -0.0031), (17.5, -0.0028), (18.0, -0.0025), (18.5, -0.0022),\n    (19.0, -0.0019), (19.5, -0.0016), (20.0, -0.0013), (20.5, -0.0010),\n    (21.0, -0.0007), (21.5, -0.0004), (22.0, -0.0004), (25.0, -0.0004),\n    (30.0, -0.0004), (40.0, -0.0004), (50.0, -0.0004), (60.0, -0.0004),\n    (70.0, -0.0003), (80.0, -0.0003), (90.0, -0.0002), (100.0, -0.0002),\n    (110.0, -0.0001), (120.0, -0.0001), (128.0, 0.0),\n]\n\n_XS = [a[0] for a in _ANCHORS]\n_YS = [a[1] for a in _ANCHORS]\n\n\ndef _interp(x):\n    # Linear interpolation on the anchor table, with linear extrapolation\n    # using the nearest two anchors at each end.\n    if x <= _XS[0]:\n        x0, y0 = _XS[0], _YS[0]\n        x1, y1 = _XS[1], _YS[1]\n        return y0 + (y1 - y0) * (x - x0) / (x1 - x0)\n    if x >= _XS[-1]:\n        x0, y0 = _XS[-2], _YS[-2]\n        x1, y1 = _XS[-1], _YS[-1]\n        return y1 + (y1 - y0) * (x - x1) / (x1 - x0)\n    # binary search\n    lo, hi = 0, len(_XS) - 1\n    while hi - lo > 1:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if _XS[mid] <= x:\n            lo = mid\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    x0, y0 = _XS[lo], _YS[lo]\n    x1, y1 = _XS[hi], _YS[hi]\n    if x1 == x0:\n        return y0\n    return y0 + (y1 - y0) * (x - x0) / (x1 - x0)\n\n\ndef predict(probe):\n    xs = probe.get(\"x\", [])\n    out = []\n    for xv in xs:\n        try:\n            x = float(xv)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError):\n            out.append(None)\n            continue\n        if math.isnan(x) or math.isinf(x):\n            out.append(None)\n            continue\n        # Clamp to the tested domain; the MLP is only defined/observed there.\n        if x < -128.0:\n            x = -128.0\n        elif x > 128.0:\n            x = 128.0\n        out.append(_interp(x))\n    return {\"y\": out}\n", "notes": "The main idea: the box is a smooth MLP approximation of a curve with a sharp positive bump around x=0 (peak \u22480.387), a shallow negative dip near |x|\u224812 (\u2248\u22120.006), and near-zero negative tails out to \u00b1128. I model it as a piecewise-linear interpolation through dense anchor points sampled from the evidence, which generalizes smoothly to unseen probes.", "since": 23, "theirs": [1.0, 1.0], "ours": [0.0, 0.5], "fit": 0.9695652173913044, "size": 2893}